@module flutter-m18 | Security
@summary Protect secrets, users and data with secure storage, HTTPS, validation and good practices.

@lesson flutter-m18-l01 | Secure storage | 8
## Learning objectives
- Store credentials safely

## Secure storage
Keep tokens in flutter_secure_storage (Keystore/Keychain), never in plain preferences or files.

```dart
import 'package:flutter_secure_storage/flutter_secure_storage.dart';

Future<String?> token() => const FlutterSecureStorage().read(key: 'token');
```

## Summary
- Secrets go in secure storage only.

@lesson flutter-m18-l02 | API key protection | 8
## Learning objectives
- Avoid leaking keys in the app

## API key protection
Anything shipped in the app can be extracted. Keep secret keys on your server, restrict public keys (by package name/SHA-1) and use `--dart-define` for config.

```dart
const apiBase = String.fromEnvironment('API_BASE', defaultValue: 'https://api.example.com');
void main() => print(apiBase);
```

## Summary
- Client apps can't hide secrets; use a backend.

@lesson flutter-m18-l03 | Authentication | 8
## Learning objectives
- Authenticate users safely

## Authentication
Use proven providers (Firebase Auth, OAuth 2/OpenID Connect with PKCE). Never store passwords locally; store short-lived tokens and refresh them.

```dart
// Prefer OAuth with PKCE via a well-reviewed package
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Don't roll your own auth crypto.

@lesson flutter-m18-l04 | Authorization | 8
## Learning objectives
- Enforce access on the server

## Authorization
The server (or security rules) must check what each user may access. Client-side checks only improve UX.

```dart
bool canEdit(String ownerId, String userId) => ownerId == userId; // UX only – enforce server-side too
void main() => print(canEdit('a', 'a'));
```

## Summary
- Authorization belongs on the server.

@lesson flutter-m18-l05 | HTTPS | 8
## Learning objectives
- Encrypt traffic

## HTTPS
Always use HTTPS. Android blocks cleartext HTTP by default on modern versions; certificate pinning adds protection for high-risk apps.

```dart
Uri api(String path) => Uri.https('api.example.com', path);
void main() => print(api('/lessons'));
```

## Summary
- Never send data over plain HTTP.

@lesson flutter-m18-l06 | Input validation | 8
## Learning objectives
- Validate and sanitise input

## Input validation
Validate on the client for UX and on the server for security. Use parameterised SQL queries and never build queries by concatenating user input.

```dart
// db.query('notes', where: 'title = ?', whereArgs: [userInput]); // safe
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Parameterise queries; validate everywhere.

@lesson flutter-m18-l07 | Data privacy | 8
## Learning objectives
- Collect less, protect more

## Data privacy
Collect only necessary data, explain it in a privacy policy, offer data export and deletion, and respect consent for analytics and ads.

```dart
// Minimise personal data; never log emails or tokens
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Privacy by design and by default.

@lesson flutter-m18-l08 | Secure networking | 8
## Learning objectives
- Harden network communication

## Secure networking
Use TLS, validate certificates, set timeouts, avoid logging sensitive headers and consider pinning for banking-grade apps.

```dart
import 'package:dio/dio.dart';

final dio = Dio(BaseOptions(connectTimeout: const Duration(seconds: 10), receiveTimeout: const Duration(seconds: 15)));
```

## Summary
- Timeouts and TLS everywhere.

@lesson flutter-m18-l09 | Common vulnerabilities | 8
## Learning objectives
- Recognise frequent mobile security mistakes

## Common vulnerabilities
OWASP Mobile Top 10 includes insecure storage, weak authentication, insufficient cryptography, insecure communication and code tampering. Obfuscate release builds and keep dependencies updated.

```dart
// flutter build apk --obfuscate --split-debug-info=build/symbols
void main() {}
```

## Summary
- Know the OWASP Mobile Top 10.
